"In 1962 four MIT students wired a PDP-1 to a modified oscilloscope and
wrote a two-player game where each player controlled a small
triangular spaceship with a thruster and a gun. The ships drifted in a
frictionless void around a central star whose gravity you had to
respect. Nine kilobytes of core memory. Two joysticks made from mercury
switches. It cost the university nothing and captured every hacker
within a three-state radius.

That game — Space War — is this chapter. You will build a ship with a
heading, a thrust, and a drift. You will discover the difference between
'the direction you are facing' and 'the direction you are moving.' You
will feel, in your thumbs, why real space is not like a road: press
forward once and you keep going forever.

Then you will add a moon, turn on `world/attract!`, and watch the ship
orbit. That is your first taste of gravitational feel — the arcade
version of Kepler's laws, at 60 frames per second.

---

## 3.1 The rotating ship

Before thrust, rotation. Left and right rotate; the ship faces where
its heading angle points.

```scheme
;; A ship is (pos vel angle): position and velocity are vecs, angle is
;; radians (0 = pointing right).
(define (ship pos vel angle) (list pos vel angle))
(define (ship-pos s) (car s))
(define (ship-vel s) (cadr s))
(define (ship-angle s) (caddr s))

;; The heading vector: an angle decomposed into (cos, sin).
(define (heading a) (vec/make (geom/cos a) (geom/sin a)))

;; Rotate the ship by d radians (left = negative, right = positive).
(define (rotate s d) (ship (ship-pos s) (ship-vel s) (+ (ship-angle s) d)))

;; Hold 'left for 5 frames from angle 0: 5 × -0.08 = -0.4 radians.
(define s0 (ship (vec/make 160 120) (vec/make 0 0) 0))
(ship-angle (fold (lambda (i s) (rotate s -0.08)) s0 (iota 5)))
;; => -0.4
```

The angle holds radians. Zero points right; pi/2 points down; pi points
left; 3pi/2 points up. `geom/cos` and `geom/sin` decompose the angle into
x- and y-components of a unit vector — that decomposition, packaged as
`heading`, IS the vector math this chapter teaches.

`fold` threads the ship through the input script, one `rotate` per frame;
the accumulator is the ship.

Hold 'left' for two seconds and the ship spins a full turn. At 0.08
radians per frame that is 60 × 0.08 = 4.8 radians per second. A full
rotation is 2×pi = 6.28 radians. So 6.28 / 4.8 = 1.3 seconds per
rotation. Do the arithmetic; feel the number.

---

## 3.2 Add thrust

Now the ship can push itself in the direction it is facing.

```scheme
;; Thrust: add an impulse in the facing direction. NO drag — this is
;; space, so velocity persists forever.
(define (thrust s amount)
  (ship (ship-pos s)
        (vec/add (ship-vel s) (vec/scale (heading (ship-angle s)) amount))
        (ship-angle s)))

;; Drift: position += velocity (no friction).
(define (drift s)
  (ship (vec/add (ship-pos s) (ship-vel s)) (ship-vel s) (ship-angle s)))

;; Face right (angle 0), thrust once, then coast three frames.
(define s1 (drift (drift (drift (thrust s0 0.15)))))
(list (ship-vel s1) (ship-pos s1))
;; => ((0.15 0) (160.45000000000002 120))
```

Read the thrust line carefully. The velocity update is the important
part:

```
(vec/add (ship-vel s) (vec/scale (heading (ship-angle s)) amount))
```

We take the current velocity. We take the heading vector `(cos ang, sin
ang)`. We scale the heading by `amount`. We add. That is vector addition.
The new velocity is the old velocity PLUS an impulse in the facing
direction.

This is why the ship drifts. Once you have accumulated `(vx=2, vy=-1)`
by thrusting up-and-to-the-right, stopping thrust does not zero those
components. The ship coasts. To stop, you rotate 180 degrees and thrust
the same amount BACKWARD to cancel the velocity. Kids figure this out in
about ninety seconds and are proud of themselves.

`drift` adds no drag: in space there is nothing to push against, so
velocity persists. To add a poor-man's friction, scale the velocity down
a hair each frame — e.g. `(vec/scale (ship-vel s) 0.98)` inside `drift` —
and the ship slowly slows without input. That 'friction' is a lie the
game tells for playability. Real Space War kept drag at zero, and pilots
hated it, and loved it.

---

## 3.3 Heading is not velocity

Here is the pedagogical crux of vector math. Draw two arrows on the
screen: one for HEADING (where the ship faces), one for VELOCITY
(where the ship is moving).

```scheme
;; Two arrows from one origin: the heading tip (where the ship faces) and
;; the velocity tip (where it moves). Return both so we can see they
;; differ. Here: thrust right once, then rotate 90° left without thrusting.
(define (arrow-tips s hlen vlen)
  (let ((p (ship-pos s)))
    (list (vec/add p (vec/scale (heading (ship-angle s)) hlen))  ;; heading
          (vec/add p (vec/scale (ship-vel s) vlen)))))           ;; velocity

;; Thrust right, coast, then turn to face straight up (angle -pi/2).
(define s-turned (rotate (thrust s0 0.15) -1.5707963267948966))
(arrow-tips s-turned 30 15)
;; => ((160 90) (162.25 120))
```

Two arrows, one origin. Rotate the ship — the heading arrow swings
around; the velocity arrow stays put. In the result, the heading tip is
straight up at `(160 90)` while the velocity tip still points right at
`(162.25 120)` — the ship faces one way and drifts another. Thrust and
the velocity arrow grows in the heading direction; rotate 180 and thrust
again and it shrinks toward zero, then flips. The kid can literally see
the vector algebra.

Appendix D (Heading vs Velocity — the Two-Arrow Diagram) formalizes this
into a poster you can tape to the wall.

---

## 3.4 Wrap-around

A ship that drifts off-screen is a lost ship. Classic arcade wraps the
world: fly off the right edge, appear on the left. Motoi has no
`world/wrap!` verb, so we roll it ourselves — it is only arithmetic on
the coordinates.

```scheme
;; Toroidal world: off-screen on the right = on-screen on the left.
;; Wrap x at 0 / 320 and y at 0 / 240, leaving velocity untouched.
(define (wrap s)
  (let* ((p (ship-pos s)) (x (car p)) (y (cadr p))
         (x2 (cond ((< x 0) (+ x 320)) ((> x 320) (- x 320)) (else x)))
         (y2 (cond ((< y 0) (+ y 240)) ((> y 240) (- y 240)) (else y))))
    (ship (vec/make x2 y2) (ship-vel s) (ship-angle s))))

;; A ship that drifted off the bottom-right reappears at the top-left.
(ship-pos (wrap (ship (vec/make 330 250) (vec/make 2 1) 0)))
;; => (10 10)
```

Two `cond`s, one per axis. Each checks both edges; if the ship crossed
one, we add or subtract the screen dimension. That is toroidal topology
in a handful of comparisons. Every Asteroids-family game does this.

Nice property: the wrap is invisible to the physics. The velocity stays
what it was — only position moves — so the ship's momentum carries
continuously across the wrap.

---

## 3.5 The moon (arcade gravity)

Now the fun part. Put a big body in the middle. Turn on gravitational
attraction.

```scheme
;; Attraction toward the moon: each frame, add a pull along the vector
;; from the ship to the moon, with the given strength. `vec/sub` gives
;; the direction, `vec/norm` its length; dividing folds in a 1/distance
;; falloff so nearer means stronger.
(define (attract s moon strength)
  (let* ((dir (vec/sub moon (ship-pos s)))
         (mag (vec/norm dir)))
    (if (= mag 0)
        s
        (ship (ship-pos s)
              (vec/add (ship-vel s) (vec/scale dir (/ strength mag)))
              (ship-angle s)))))

;; Ship at (240 120), moon at (160 120), kicked straight up (vy -2.2).
;; One frame of attract + drift curves it toward the moon.
(define moon (vec/make 160 120))
(define orbiting (drift (attract (ship (vec/make 240 120)
                                       (vec/make 0 -2.2) 0)
                                 moon 0.15)))
(ship-pos orbiting)
;; => (239.85 117.8)
```

`attract` says: every frame, add a pull toward the moon's position, with
strength 0.15. The number is not proper Newtonian G, but dividing by
distance is falloff-like enough that orbits behave.

The initial velocity `(vec/make 0 -2.2)` kicks the ship straight up (y
negative = up). With the moon pulling left (moon at 160, ship at 240),
the ship curves left as it climbs — you can see it in the result, x
dropping from 240 to 239.85 while it moves up. Tune the tangential speed
right and it enters a stable ellipse.

Try different initial y-velocities: 1.0 falls in; 2.2 orbits; 5.0
escapes. That is the elementary calculation of orbital energy — total
energy = kinetic + potential; if it exceeds zero the ship escapes.
Kids do not need to know the formula to feel it.

The ship's own thrust is available; a good pilot can adjust orbit
apogee and perigee by thrusting prograde (in the direction of motion)
or retrograde (against). This is the same mechanic astronauts use to
change orbit.

---

## 3.6 The four patterns in a space game

**Composition.** The ship is composed: position, velocity, angle — and
`attract`, `thrust`, `drift`, `wrap` are the small functions that act on
those parts. The moon is just a target point. Chapters 4-5 will compose
bullets, enemies, health.

**Orchestration.** The frame loop orchestrates: rotate on heading input,
thrust on thrust input, `attract`, `drift`, then `wrap`. Note that wrap
happens AFTER drift; if we wrapped before moving, the ship would
sometimes teleport mid-frame and momentum could look wrong.

**In-routing.** Three inputs route to three functions: left rotates,
right rotates, 'a' thrusts. Later ('b' fires) adds a fourth. Each is a
small pure function returning a new ship.

**Routing.** `attract` is the force router: each frame it routes a pull
from the moon into the ship's velocity. If we had many moons and many
ships, we would `fold` `attract` over every moon for each ship.

---

## 3.7 Common desktop tasks

**Ship-tourism scoreboard.** A shop selling telescopes or space-themed
products embeds a small ship-and-moon minigame: pilot from Earth to
Moon in the fewest thrusts. Best time gets a display banner. The game
teaches orbital economy while it sells the product.

**Package-drift receipt animation.** After checkout, a small ship
carries the buyer's package sprite across the screen, drifting past
planets that represent the item's category. Physics-first branding.

---

## 3.8 Pitfalls

**The ship spins but does not move.** Your `thrust` did not feed the new
velocity back into the ship. Check that `ship-vel` is a vec `(vx vy)` and
that `thrust` returns a ship built with the updated velocity.

**The ship stops as soon as you release 'a'.** Your `drift` scales the
velocity down. Space has no drag. Leave velocity untouched unless you
want a poor-man's Newton's-first-law violation.

**The orbit spirals in.** Your initial tangential velocity is too low
— the ship falls into the moon. Increase the kick to `(vec/make 0 -2.5)`
and try again.

**The orbit spirals out.** Initial velocity too high — escape
trajectory. Decrease.

**Rotation is jerky.** `0.08` radians per frame is a fine baseline;
below 0.04 feels slow, above 0.15 is spinny. Consider using
`(easing/apply 'ease-out t)` to ramp rotation speed in the frames after
button press, so the ship 'settles into' its rotation rate. Chapter 6
(Feel) shows exactly this.

**Coordinate confusion.** In screen space, y grows DOWN. `geom/sin
angle` returns positive values for angles in the second and third
quadrants (angle between 0 and pi radians), which point DOWN on the
screen. If your ship rotates the wrong way, remember: screen-y is
flipped from math-y.

---

## 3.9 Try this yourself

1. Add a second moon. Call `attract` for both moons each frame (fold
   over a list of moons). Watch the ship figure-eight between them
   (three-body problem, in the small).
2. Add a constant solar wind by folding `(vec/make 0.05 0)` into the
   velocity in `drift`. The ship drifts sideways without input. Does that
   ruin the game or make it more interesting?
3. Cap the drift: clamp the velocity's `vec/norm` to 5 inside `drift`.
   Space with a speed limit is not-space, but it plays gently.
4. Flip the sign of `strength` in `attract` — the moon becomes a shield
   generator that repels. Fly around it; try to get close.
5. Add a fire input: each press appends a bullet whose velocity =
   ship-velocity + heading × bullet-speed. Chapter 4 covers this in
   depth.
6. Make the wrap fade in on the new edge — record a ghost at the old
   position that fades over 15 frames.

---

## 3.10 What we built

A ship. A heading. A thrust. A drift. A wrap. A moon. Vector
decomposition into cos-and-sin components. `attract` as arcade gravity.
Two-arrow visualization of heading versus velocity.

Next chapter: the ship stops moving, it stays put, but it FIRES. We
build cannons and projectiles. `phys/kinematics` finally earns its
name.")

(code-block :chapter 3 :lang "scheme" :source
"(define (ship pos vel angle) (list pos vel angle))
(define (ship-pos s) (car s))
(define (ship-vel s) (cadr s))
(define (ship-angle s) (caddr s))
(define (heading a) (vec/make (geom/cos a) (geom/sin a)))
(define (rotate s d) (ship (ship-pos s) (ship-vel s) (+ (ship-angle s) d)))
(define (thrust s amount)
  (ship (ship-pos s)
        (vec/add (ship-vel s) (vec/scale (heading (ship-angle s)) amount))
        (ship-angle s)))
(define (drift s)
  (ship (vec/add (ship-pos s) (ship-vel s)) (ship-vel s) (ship-angle s)))
(define (wrap s)
  (let* ((p (ship-pos s)) (x (car p)) (y (cadr p))
         (x2 (cond ((< x 0) (+ x 320)) ((> x 320) (- x 320)) (else x)))
         (y2 (cond ((< y 0) (+ y 240)) ((> y 240) (- y 240)) (else y))))
    (ship (vec/make x2 y2) (ship-vel s) (ship-angle s))))
(define (ship-and-thrust-anchor frames)
  (let loop ((s (ship (vec/make 160 120) (vec/make 0 0) 0)) (n frames))
    (if (= n 0)
        (ship-pos s)
        (loop (wrap (drift (thrust s 0.15))) (- n 1)))))
(ship-and-thrust-anchor 30)")
